---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * WRITTEN FROM AN AUDIT OF THE SITE, NOT FROM A TEMPLATE. Before changing it,
 * check the change is still true of the actual site. As of October 2026:
 *   - no analytics, advertising, first-party cookies, localStorage or
 *     database (grep for gtag / analytics / document.cookie / localStorage:
 *     all absent, and confirmed by loading every page in a real browser)
 *   - BUT embedded Google content sets THIRD-PARTY cookies: /forms/membership
 *     leaves S + COMPASS (docs.google.com) and NID (google.com), /calendar
 *     leaves NID. Measured with a fresh cookie jar per page. Re-measure before
 *     editing the Cookies section; do not take this on trust.
 *   - Google Fonts on all 25 pages
 *   - Google Forms embedded on 3 pages, Microsoft Forms on 5
 *   - Google Calendar embedded on /calendar
 *   - meeting slide PDFs are served from this site, not a third party
 *
 * If somebody adds analytics or a new embed, this page has to change with it.
 */
import Navbar from '../components/home/navbar.jsx';
import Footer from '../components/home/footer.jsx';
import '../components/css/pages.css';

const updated = 'October 19, 2026';
const email = 'jchsfbla@gmail.com';
---

<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Privacy Policy | Johns Creek FBLA</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content="What the Johns Creek FBLA website does and does not collect about visitors." />
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; background-color: #f7f7f7; font-family: 'Oswald', sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <Navbar client:load />
  <main class="page legal">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        Last updated {updated}. This describes what the Johns Creek FBLA
        website does with information about the people who visit it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>The short version</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        We do not collect anything about you. There is no account to make, no
        analytics, and no advertising. This site sets no cookies of its own on
        any page. Two pages embed something from Google, and on those two
        Google sets cookies of its own: the detail is below, with the page
        names and the cookie names.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Who runs this site</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Johns Creek FBLA, the student chapter at Johns Creek High School in
        Johns Creek, Georgia. Chapter officers maintain it. It is a student-run
        site, not an official publication of Fulton County Schools or of FBLA.
        Questions go to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>What we collect</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Nothing. Every page here is a static file. There is no login, no
        database, no analytics or advertising script, and no cookie set by this
        site. We have no way to tell who visited, what they read, or how often.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>What our host records</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub's documentation states:
        &ldquo;When a GitHub Pages site is visited, the visitor's IP address is
        logged and stored for security purposes, regardless of whether the
        visitor has signed into GitHub or not.&rdquo; Those logs belong to
        GitHub. We never see them and cannot request them.
      </p>
      <p class="panel-body">
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Pages documentation</a>
        &middot;
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub privacy statement</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header">
        <h2>Content loaded from other companies</h2>
        <p>Some of what you see on a page is fetched from somebody else's server.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="legal-list">
        <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong>, on every page, for the typeface.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Forms</strong>, embedded on our membership and sign-up pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Microsoft Forms</strong>, embedded on several conference pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Calendar</strong>, embedded on the calendar page.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Whenever your browser loads something from another company's server,
        that request tells that server your IP address and basic details about
        your browser. That is how the web works, and it happens whether or not
        you interact with what loaded. What those companies do with it is
        covered by their policies rather than this one:
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google</a>
        and
        <a href="https://privacy.microsoft.com/en-us/privacystatement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="panel-body">
        The meeting slide PDFs are the exception. Those are served from this
        site, so opening one involves nobody else.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header">
        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>Checked by loading each page in a browser with an empty cookie jar.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        This site sets no cookies and uses no browser storage anywhere. Most
        pages finish loading with none at all.
      </p>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Two pages are different, because of what they embed:
      </p>
      <ul class="legal-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The membership form page</strong> embeds a Google Form, and
          loading it leaves three Google cookies in your browser, named
          <code>S</code> and <code>COMPASS</code> on docs.google.com, and
          <code>NID</code> on google.com.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The calendar page</strong> embeds Google Calendar and leaves
          one, <code>NID</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Those are Google's cookies, not ours. We did not ask for them, we cannot
        read them, and they arrive whether or not you type anything into the
        form. Google explains what they are for in its
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/cookies" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cookie documentation</a>.
        If you would rather not have them, you can read the same information
        without the embed: the calendar and every form are also linked
        directly, and your browser's settings can block third-party cookies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>If you fill out a form</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        Opening the page loads the form from Google or Microsoft, as described
        above. Your answers are a separate thing: nothing is sent until you type
        them in and press submit. When you do:
      </p>
      <ul class="legal-list">
        <li>Your answers go to Google or Microsoft, whichever hosts that form.</li>
        <li>Chapter officers and our faculty advisor can see the responses.</li>
        <li>We use them to run the chapter: membership, event sign-ups, competition registration, and contacting you about those.</li>
        <li>We do not sell responses and we do not give them to advertisers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="panel-body">
        If you want a response deleted, email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>,
        say which form it was, and we will remove it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Payments</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        No payment is ever taken on this website. Payment links send you to
        Fulton County Schools' SchoolCash Online, which is the district's system
        and runs under the district's terms. We never see a card number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Photos of members</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        We publish photos of chapter members at meetings, conferences and
        events. If you are in one, or you are the parent or guardian of a
        student in one, and you want it taken down, email
        <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>. We will remove it. You do not
        have to explain why.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Links to other sites</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        We link to Georgia FBLA, FBLA, Instagram, Linktree, Snap! Raise,
        SchoolCash Online and various Google documents. Once you follow a link
        you are on their site, under their rules, and this policy stops
        applying.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Children</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        This site is meant for high school students, their families, and our
        community. Since we collect nothing directly from any visitor, we
        collect nothing from children either.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-header"><h2>Changes to this page</h2></div>
      <p class="panel-body">
        If the site changes in a way that makes something here untrue, we update
        this page and the date at the top. Every edit this site has ever had,
        this page included, is public in our
        <a href="https://github.com/JohnsCreekFBLA/johnscreekfbla.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub repository</a>,
        so you can check what changed and when.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="page-footnote">
      Questions or requests: <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer client:load />
</body>
</html>
